                <h4>🇬🇧 English</h4>
                <p>This piece portrays the Buddha’s teaching as mobile, human-centered education. Rather than delivering a one-size-fits-all doctrine, he relies on <i>upāya</i>—skillful means—tailoring guidance to people’s capacities and circumstances. In Sarnath, the Four Noble Truths become a practical map of suffering; elsewhere he meets ascetics, merchants, rulers, skeptics, and even criminals, helping each person identify the specific attachment that keeps them trapped.</p>
                <p>The emphasis is not on distant sermons but on lived instruction: stories, analogies, and staged experiences that make insight tangible. Even the Buddha’s final months are framed as a teaching on impermanence—less a gloomy verdict than an invitation to live with clarity, compassion, and less grasping.</p>
